      objectives: ["u10-o1"],
      html: `<p class="lead">You transmit one frequency; the body sends back that one plus faint copies at whole-number multiples of it.</p>
<p>Pluck a guitar string and you hear one obvious note, but the string is also vibrating at two times, three times and four times that rate. Those extra tones are the <span class="kw">harmonics</span>, and they give the note its character. Ultrasound in tissue does the same thing.</p>
<p>The frequency the transducer transmits is the <span class="kw">fundamental frequency</span>, often written f<sub>0</sub>. The useful harmonic in imaging is the <span class="kw">second harmonic</span> — exactly <b>twice</b> the fundamental.</p>
<div class="formula">second harmonic = 2 × fundamental</div>
<p>So a 2 MHz transmit pulse produces a 4 MHz harmonic; 3 MHz gives 6 MHz; 1.5 MHz gives 3 MHz. Read it backwards for the other exam flavour: if the system is listening at 7 MHz for the second harmonic, it transmitted 3.5 MHz. Higher harmonics (third = 3×, fourth = 4×) exist but are far too weak to image with.</p>
<p>Size matters here: the harmonic signal is <b>much weaker</b> than the fundamental — on the order of <b>10–20 dB down</b>, meaning tens of times smaller in amplitude. That is why harmonic imaging needed modern broadband transducers and sensitive electronics before it became routine.</p>
<p>Two completely different sources create harmonics in clinical scanning:</p>
<ul>
<li><span class="kw-2">Tissue harmonics</span> — generated by the tissue itself as the sound wave travels (next lesson). No injection needed.</li>
<li><span class="kw-2">Contrast harmonics</span> — generated by injected gas microbubbles ringing non-linearly.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout key">Fundamental = what you send. Second harmonic = 2 × fundamental = what you listen for. Harmonic echoes are weak, roughly 10–20 dB below the fundamental.</div>
<div class="callout tip">Dumb saying: <b>"Talk low, listen high."</b> Transmit the low fundamental, receive the doubled harmonic.</div>`,
