      objectives: ["u10-o3"],
      html: `<p class="lead">Strong in the middle of the beam, absent at the skin, building with depth and then fading as attenuation wins.</p>
<p>Two rules decide where tissue harmonics exist: they need <b>distance</b> to accumulate and <b>high intensity</b> to be generated at all. Put those together and the map of harmonic strength looks like this.</p>
<p><b>Across the beam.</b> Intensity is highest along the <b>central axis</b>; it falls off toward the edges and falls off dramatically in the side lobes and grating lobes. Since harmonic generation depends steeply on intensity, harmonics are <b>strongest in the centre of the beam</b> and nearly absent at the weak edges. Effectively, the harmonic beam is <b>narrower</b> than the fundamental beam — which is a free improvement in lateral resolution.</p>
<p><b>With depth.</b> At the transducer face the pulse has had no distance to distort, so the harmonic amplitude is essentially <b>zero in the very near field</b>. Harmonic content then <b>builds with depth</b>, peaks somewhere around the focal region, and eventually <b>falls off deep</b> because the harmonic is a high frequency and attenuation punishes high frequencies hardest (attenuation in soft tissue ≈ 0.5 dB per cm per MHz — double the frequency and you double the dB per cm).</p>
<p>That fading at depth is the honest limitation of harmonic imaging: <b>penetration is reduced</b> compared with fundamental imaging at the same transmit frequency.</p>
<div class="callout key">Harmonics: none at the transducer face → build with depth → strongest near the focus and along the central axis → fade deep because of attenuation.</div>
<div class="callout tip">Dumb saying: <b>"Centre stage, mid-show."</b> Harmonics perform in the middle of the beam, in the middle of the depth range.</div>
<div class="callout warn">Trap: "harmonics are strongest at the skin surface" is always wrong — that is where they are weakest. Also wrong: "harmonics improve deep penetration". They improve <i>clarity</i>, at the cost of penetration.</div>`,
