      html: `<p class="lead">Almost every source of junk in an ultrasound image is a fundamental-frequency phenomenon — so listening at 2f<sub>0</sub> deletes it.</p>
<p>Think about what makes a hard-to-scan abdomen look bad: haze in the near field, clutter filling the gallbladder or bladder, reverberation from the body wall, and ghost structures written in from side and grating lobes. Harmonic imaging attacks all of them at once, for one reason: <b>those artifacts are carried by the fundamental frequency, and the receiver is no longer listening there.</b></p>
<table class="dt"><tr><th>Benefit</th><th>Why it happens</th></tr>
<tr><td>Fewer side-lobe and grating-lobe artifacts</td><td>Lobes are low-intensity, so they never generate harmonics</td></tr>
<tr><td>Less reverberation and near-field clutter</td><td>Harmonics have not formed yet in the first centimetre or two</td></tr>
<tr><td>Better lateral resolution</td><td>The harmonic beam is narrower than the fundamental beam</td></tr>
<tr><td>Better axial resolution</td><td>Higher frequency → shorter wavelength and spatial pulse length</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cleaner cystic structures, better contrast resolution</td><td>Clutter filling fluid spaces is removed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Especially helpful in technically difficult (large) patients</td><td>Body-wall clutter is exactly what harmonics suppress</td></tr></table>
<p>The costs are real too. <b>Penetration is reduced</b>, because the returning harmonic is high frequency and weak to begin with. It demands a <span class="kw">wide-bandwidth (broadband) transducer</span> — the probe must be able to transmit at f<sub>0</sub> <i>and</i> receive efficiently at 2f<sub>0</sub>, which a narrow-bandwidth, high-Q probe simply cannot do. And when pulse inversion is used to isolate the harmonic, <b>frame rate drops</b> because two pulses are fired per scan line.</p>
<div class="callout key">Harmonic imaging: less lobe artifact, less reverberation/clutter, thinner beam, better lateral and axial resolution — at the cost of penetration, and requiring a broadband transducer.</div>
<div class="callout tip">Dumb saying: <b>"Twice the frequency, half the junk."</b></div>`,
